<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/072R">Remark <span data-tag="072R">9.7.8.14</span>.</a> We will be particularly interested in the situation where $\mathbb {K}$ is the collection of all $\kappa $-small simplicial sets, for some regular cardinal $\kappa $. In this case, we say that an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a <em>$\kappa $-cocomplete idempotent</em> if it is a $\mathbb {K}$-cocomplete idempotent, in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="072P" href="/tag/072P">9.7.8.12</a>. In the special case $\kappa = \aleph _0$, we say instead that $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a <em>finitely cocomplete idempotent</em>. In the special case where $\mathbb {K}$ is the collection of all small simplicial sets, we say instead that $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a <em>cocomplete idempotent</em>. Note that an $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}$ is a presentable idempotent (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="072C" href="/tag/072C">9.7.8.1</a>) if and only if it is both presentable and a cocomplete idempotent. </p>
